WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:13.629
We may not like it, but we can’t say that
corruption in Argentina – as in most countries

00:00:13.629 --> 00:00:14.680
– is new.

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:19.650
The truth is that since the first government
meeting, back in 1810, virtually no Argentine

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:24.420
government has been free of suspicion.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:31.669
Yep... the possibilities are almost endless...
and the incentives, very generous.

00:00:31.669 --> 00:00:36.930
And don’t think it's just a matter of sticking
your hand in and you’re done, most corruption

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:39.440
cases are much more convoluted.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:44.270
However, folks, what has been uncovered in
recent years goes much further.

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:49.010
We’re talking about the realization that
in Argentina there has been a systematic process,

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:53.429
led by the nation's own government, with the
objective of looting the country's public

00:00:53.429 --> 00:00:56.469
treasury on a rather large scale.

00:00:56.469 --> 00:01:02.940
And… we’re not talking about the occasional
bribe… but of billions of dollars that,

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:07.030
given the evidence, seem to have been stolen
during the Peronist governments of Néstor

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:08.570
Kirchner and Cristina Fernández de Kirchner.

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:16.230
The news comes almost daily, but this video’s
goal is to tell you how it all started, what

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:20.620
happened, and how this plot worked-- a plot
which the very Presidency of the Republic

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:26.210
was involved... as well as dozens of politicians,
judges, officials and many of the most important

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:29.100
businessmen in the country.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:34.920
Folks, this looting operation’s plot, known
as the "notebooks", began with the arrival

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:39.890
of Néstor Kirchner in May 2003 and continued
under the presidency of his wife, Cristina

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:42.590
Fernández de Kirchner until 2015.

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:46.360
That is to say, the systematic pillaging of
the public coffers happened for more than

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:47.360
a decade.

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:51.600
Now, before we look into what this consisted
of and how much money it meant for the Argentines,

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:57.640
we have to answer some questions about the
real protagonists of this story: the Kirchners.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:01:58.640
Who are they?

00:01:58.640 --> 00:01:59.640
How did they get into power?

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:03.660
How did they manage to set what is perhaps
the biggest operation in Argentina's history

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:10.880
of public coffers looting into motion?

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:14.210
Listen up.

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:19.730
(AUDIO: THE KIRCHNER’S ASCENT)

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:23.439
Two provincial politicians who managed to
own Argentina for years.

00:02:23.439 --> 00:02:28.620
Folks, it all began with a beautiful love
story between two young university students.

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:33.569
Néstor and Cristina met in the mid-70s while
studying law at the National University of

00:02:33.569 --> 00:02:35.390
La Plata in Buenos Aires.

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:41.400
Love soon bloomed and in 1976, after the military
coup led by Lieutenant General Jorge Rafael

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:46.370
Videla, our two lovers decided to leave to
work as lawyers in the city of Río Gallegos

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:48.319
[GA-YE-GOS], in the province of Santa Cruz.

00:02:48.319 --> 00:02:56.389
A city that by the way is far away from the
capital, more than 2,650 km away!

00:02:56.389 --> 00:03:00.599
If we’d been asked at that time about this
couple’s political future, we probably wouldn’t

00:03:00.599 --> 00:03:03.330
have mentioned national politics.

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:07.790
However... after democracy returned in 1983,
this nice lawyer couple joined the Peronist

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:13.400
Party... and it was there folks, that the
career of those who became Argentina’s Underwoods

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:14.400
really began...

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:15.400
Well, now that I think about it...

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:19.519
I think it was the Underwoods who became the
Kirchners of the political series.

00:03:19.519 --> 00:03:22.499
A relationship made by and for power.

00:03:22.499 --> 00:03:27.909
First, in 1987 Néstor Kirchner was elected
mayor of Río Gallegos, and a few years later,

00:03:27.909 --> 00:03:30.890
in 1991, Governor of the Province of Santa
Cruz.

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:36.560
Then finally, in 2003, he became President
of the Republic of Argentina.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:41.689
And during this time, Cristina Fernández
didn’t stay on the sidelines.

00:03:41.689 --> 00:03:46.409
She made her career in the legislative chambers,
as provincial deputy, national deputy and

00:03:46.409 --> 00:03:51.260
national senator... until in 2007, she arrived
at the Presidency of the Republic.

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:55.310
And... then, in 2015, she went back to the
Senate.

00:03:55.310 --> 00:04:00.319
Yes, it seems that the Kirchners had it very,
very clear: between the legal profession and

00:04:00.319 --> 00:04:01.319
politics...

00:04:01.319 --> 00:04:02.319
Politics.

00:04:02.319 --> 00:04:05.010
More than 30 years living off of taxpayers.

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:08.349
Now that's a career...

00:04:08.349 --> 00:04:15.169
However... just a moment.

00:04:15.169 --> 00:04:25.240
The 
province of Santa Cruz, which is where the

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:29.319
Kirchners began their political career isn’t
only far from the capital, which after all

00:04:29.319 --> 00:04:36.160
is the center of power, but in 2001 its population
didn’t reach 200,000 inhabitants... it was

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:41.520
about 0.5% of the country’s entire population,
which at the time was approximately 36 million

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:42.870
people.

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:44.210
So...

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:49.091
How did two politicians from a place that
is so far from the core of power and is so

00:04:49.091 --> 00:04:52.050
small take control of the entire country?

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:55.830
This’s the question that many journalists
and political analysts have been asking for

00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:56.980
a long time.

00:04:56.980 --> 00:04:59.520
Well... now we’re beginning to unravel the
answer.

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:05.229
Basically, this husband and wife took over,
co-opted and bought almost every sector of

00:05:05.229 --> 00:05:18.620
Argentine politics through the power of their
wallets:

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:26.889
Businessmen, trade unionists, judges, journalists,
artists and civil society leaders succumbed

00:05:26.889 --> 00:05:29.260
to the power of the state's wallet.

00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:34.150
Billions of dollars distributed among the
pockets of those who supported and were unconditional

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:36.180
supporters of this peculiar marriage.

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:42.530
A clientele network fed and greased by concessions,
aid and subsidies that were conveniently distributed

00:05:42.530 --> 00:05:43.970
and which nobody controlled.

00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:44.970
However….

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:47.770
The question that we have to ask ourselves
is...

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:48.919
How did they finance it?

00:05:48.919 --> 00:05:57.099
How did the Kirchners maintain such an ample
clientelistic group?

00:05:57.099 --> 00:06:03.150
Listen up.

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:07.889
(THE GOAL OF PUBLIC WORK)

00:06:07.889 --> 00:06:12.680
Look at what Cristina Fernández said in 2010:

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:18.500
( “For us, for this Administration, public
work isn’t a contingency, it’s a conviction,

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:24.479
a structural political concept that we’ve
been developing not only since 2003, but since

00:06:24.479 --> 00:06:28.699
we gained the responsibility of directing
a community’s destiny”.

00:06:28.699 --> 00:06:30.659
Cristina Fernández de Kirchner.)

00:06:30.659 --> 00:06:34.669
Well, now we know why public works were so
important for this marriage...

00:06:34.669 --> 00:06:38.590
And no, I'm sorry, but it has nothing to do
with Argentina’s interests.

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:42.979
Public work was one of the core parts of the
Kirchners’ political fabric, first in the

00:06:42.979 --> 00:06:45.879
province of Santa Cruz and then throughout
the country.

00:06:45.879 --> 00:06:50.290
Now... some of you may be wondering, but,
Simon, How did the system work?

00:06:50.290 --> 00:06:54.080
Well... you’ll see… deep down it was quite
simple.

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:57.980
President Kirchner, through his Minister of
Federal Planning and Public Investment, Julio

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:01.379
[HOO-LEE-O] de Vido [VEE-DO] – who stayed
in this post for the entire period in which

00:07:01.379 --> 00:07:05.980
the Kirchners headed the country – met periodically
with the main construction companies in Argentina,

00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:10.580
which in turn were convened by the President
of the Chamber of Construction, Carlos Wagner.

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:15.250
Well, in these meetings it was agreed exactly
which company would get each public tender...

00:07:15.250 --> 00:07:20.580
That is, they met to decide how to distribute
the ‘cake’ .

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:28.139
Then... when everything was decided the tender
procedure started... but it was for mere appearances…

00:07:28.139 --> 00:07:32.490
the company which was chosen was the one that
this kind of “public works cartel” had

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:35.360
previously agreed on.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:39.180
And now comes the key to the entire process...

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:43.509
Once the work was granted, the companies charged
the first installment of the contract, the

00:07:43.509 --> 00:07:47.889
advance payment they needed to supposedly
begin working… however, this payment was

00:07:47.889 --> 00:08:00.340
given entirely to

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:02.969
the politicians.

00:08:02.969 --> 00:08:08.060
So wait.. where was the business if these
companies had to pay back what they charged?

00:08:08.060 --> 00:08:11.219
Well... in the extra costs.

00:08:11.219 --> 00:08:19.090
See, it’s estimated that on average each
project’s extra cost ranged between 30 and

00:08:19.090 --> 00:08:20.830
40 percent of the total bill.

00:08:20.830 --> 00:08:25.379
That way, if work was granted for 100 million,
budget expansions were approved for up to

00:08:25.379 --> 00:08:27.849
a total of approximately 140 million.

00:08:27.849 --> 00:08:28.849
But that's not all.

00:08:28.849 --> 00:08:32.800
Sometimes the government would also approve
changes and new project expansions… until

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:37.450
the company that had been awarded the 100
million dollar work ended up charging a lot

00:08:37.450 --> 00:08:38.450
more...

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:44.700
180, 200, 250, and even 2.8 times the initial
budget, so in this example, 280 million...

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:49.529
And to top it all off, many of these projects
were not even finished.

00:08:49.529 --> 00:08:50.529
You heard that right.

00:08:50.529 --> 00:08:55.040
Of course, in exchange for this bargain, companies
paid bribes to politicians--depending on the

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:57.800
case, between 10 and 20% of the invoiced amount.

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:01.250
Having said that, have you ever wondered how
public work functions in your country?

00:09:01.250 --> 00:09:04.820
The case, folks, is that this whole process
worked perfectly for years...

00:09:04.820 --> 00:09:14.920
But now... it’s been exposed.

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:18.200
(DISCOVERED)

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:35.139
Folks, the

00:09:35.139 --> 00:09:36.970
little details count...

00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:41.470
When one doesn’t take care of all the details
even the most complex and ingenious operations

00:09:41.470 --> 00:09:43.060
run the risk of collapsing.

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:45.120
This case wasn’t the exception.

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:49.970
See, it turns out that the driver who transported
the government’s “bribe collector” was

00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:54.130
obsessed with writing down each trip, each
stop, each building visited, the name of the

00:09:54.130 --> 00:09:58.220
people he was going to see for the briefcase
delivery... even the conversations he heard

00:09:58.220 --> 00:09:59.220
during his travels.

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:02.560
He’d written down practically everything
in 8 notebooks.

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:07.480
We don’t know if he did it on impulse, for
entertainment, if it was part of a kind of

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:11.810
control and accounting system or he simply
wanted to cover his back... but the fact is

00:10:11.810 --> 00:10:17.990
that these notebooks contain all the details
of this mafia plot.

00:10:17.990 --> 00:10:21.949
And finally those notebooks... came into the
hands of a journalist who, upon seeing what

00:10:21.949 --> 00:10:26.070
they contained, decided to turn them over
to justice... and after months of investigation,

00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:31.490
at the end of July 2018, high officials and
businessmen linked to the plot were arrested.

00:10:31.490 --> 00:10:36.070
But that's not all... now many of these people
have decided to collaborate with the justice

00:10:36.070 --> 00:10:40.839
department in exchange for penitentiary benefits...
such as not being in prison while the process

00:10:40.839 --> 00:10:41.839
continues.

00:10:41.839 --> 00:10:45.750
And one of those individuals is, Carlos Wagner,
the President of the Chamber of Construction,

00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:49.370
the man who called the companies and one of
the people who benefited most from this entire

00:10:49.370 --> 00:10:50.370
mess.

00:10:50.370 --> 00:10:52.320
His story confirmed everything the notebooks
said.

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:54.529
And the other testimonies went in the same
line.

00:10:54.529 --> 00:10:55.529
But... right now...

00:10:55.529 --> 00:10:59.130
I'm sure you may be wondering, OK, OK, Simon,
but how much money are we talking about...

00:10:59.130 --> 00:11:02.209
Well, folks, a lot-- and I mean a lot--of
money.

00:11:02.209 --> 00:11:04.390
See, if Carlos Wagner is telling the truth...

00:11:04.390 --> 00:11:08.300
The Kirchners would’ve received almost 3
million dollars... 3 MILLION DOLLARS A DAY

00:11:08.300 --> 00:11:13.190
during their 12 and a half years in government!!

00:11:13.190 --> 00:11:17.630
That is, more than 12 billion dollars in total,
only from public works.

00:11:17.630 --> 00:11:24.050
Because, besides that, there are many more
suspicions of corruption and pending cases.

00:11:24.050 --> 00:11:30.450
12 billion to pay politicians, judges, journalists,
social leaders... and, of course, to fill

00:11:30.450 --> 00:11:31.760
their pockets.

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:35.699
Everything indicates that this is how the
Kirchners came to power and consolidated that

00:11:35.699 --> 00:11:37.810
power over Argentina.

00:11:37.810 --> 00:11:43.709
For example, there’s also José López’s
case, former public works secretary and someone

00:11:43.709 --> 00:11:49.070
who was very close to Julio de Vido, the Kirchnerist
minister who managed the plot.

00:11:49.070 --> 00:11:53.339
Well, in June 2016, having been alerted that
the police could raid his house as part of

00:11:53.339 --> 00:11:58.380
an investigation… he decided to leave at
3 in the morning with 9 million dollars he

00:11:58.380 --> 00:12:00.070
had in cash... hidden under the mattress.

00:12:00.070 --> 00:12:04.220
Anyway, that night López tried to hide the
money in a convent-- the convent of the Praying

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:09.180
and Penitent Nuns of Our Lady of the Rosary
of Fatima – but didn’t have the best luck.

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:12.740
While he was stashing the money, a neighbor,
who thought that it was a robbery, called

00:12:12.740 --> 00:12:13.740
the police.

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:16.410
And that's how good old Jose Lopez was caught
red-handed.

00:12:16.410 --> 00:12:23.259
Now we know where all that money probably
came from.

00:12:23.259 --> 00:12:27.040
By the way, as you can imagine, this money
couldn’t simply be transferred to a bank...

00:12:27.040 --> 00:12:31.310
No, it had to be paid in cash so it wouldn’t
leave a trace... so it was moved in bags.

00:12:31.310 --> 00:12:35.020
Well, since they made so many payments…
the people in charge of filling the bags already

00:12:35.020 --> 00:12:37.490
knew how many money wads fit into each bag...

00:12:37.490 --> 00:12:45.519
So it seems that the money was no longer counted
in millions, but by the number of bags.

00:12:45.519 --> 00:12:50.190
By the way, don’t forget that companies
also made money by overpricing... so the damage

00:12:50.190 --> 00:12:52.199
to the Argentine people has been even greater...

00:12:52.199 --> 00:12:56.149
Anyway, folks, this is the gruesome story
of how some politicians decided to plunder

00:12:56.149 --> 00:12:57.149
their own country.

00:12:57.149 --> 00:13:02.110
If the facts are confirmed, even the Underwoods
would be scandalized by Argentina’s situation.

00:13:02.110 --> 00:13:04.550
Will Cristina Fernández end up in jail?

00:13:04.550 --> 00:13:05.680
Only time will tell.

